{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>If the sun has risen upon him.</b> That is, that he entered there by day, or that he lingered there until morning, when the sun had spread itself in the world." "<b>He has blood-guilt.</b> There is a liability of death upon anyone who kills him, because it is not found that he came for matters of life-and-death, and surely he would not kill, for he already knows that everyone knows about him and will seize him for execution. And Scripture used the feminine form, ״אם זרחה השמש״, and did not say ״אם זרח״, because so is the way of Scripture to use the feminine form when it wishes to speak of the time when the sun spreads through the world and expands across the breadth of its expanse; and so (II Samuel 12:11): ״לעיני השמש הזאת״. But when it begins to shine and wishes to emerge and flash in its radiance, it is called in the masculine form, as in (Genesis 32:32): ״ויזרח לו השמש״, and it is written (ibid. 19:23): ״השמש יצא על הארץ״, and it is written (Psalms 19:6): ״ישיש כגבור לרוץ אורח״. Therefore it used here the feminine form, to say that if the sun had spread in the world — he knows he will be caught and cannot escape their hands — ״דמים לו״: he has blood-guilt for whoever kills him, yet he must make restitution for what he stole; if he has nothing, he shall be sold for his theft."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:02:53.008986345Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}